{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"d64e39b62422977b","type":"text","text":"# Planting Ivy\n\nVerena shows me small cuttings in her workshop, explaining that she is growing ivy to decorate and support a dying tree on a grave. She explains that by doing this, she won't need to cut it down.\n\n![Planting Ivy](2026-06-02-planting-ivy.jpeg)\n\n","x":320,"y":1436,"width":380,"height":854},
		{"id":"f01a802c7ce04d08","type":"text","text":"# Trimming the ivy\n\nSandrine and Verena are trimming the ivy at the base of the tree trunk. This is both for aesthetic reasons and to allow the tree to breathe.\n\n![Trimming the ivy](2026-06-02-trimming-the-ivy.jpeg)","x":-300,"y":1176,"width":380,"height":815},
		{"id":"865236c05b2a2bab","type":"text","text":"# Grave Butterflies\n\nA grave is covered with small black pebbles. A butterfly sits on the stones. It looks as if it is there to die, and turn into a pebble itself. This story was told by Sabrina.\n\n![Grave Butterflies](2026-06-02-grave-butterfly.jpeg)","x":-440,"y":260,"width":380,"height":854,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"9dd1f0836702ae0f","type":"text","text":"# Portal Trees\n\nDuring my first meeting with Sandrine and Verena, the gardeners at the Cemetery of Kings, Verena told me about the myth surrounding the Cedars, which, in some beliefs, represent a gateway between the world of the living and the world of the dead.\n\n![Portal Trees](2026-06-02-portal-tree.jpeg)\n","x":800,"y":1128,"width":380,"height":911,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"47b7fad8d75b0f29","type":"text","text":"# The Cemetery Meadows\n\nIn the spring, Verena explains to me that they create what they call meadows, leaving some areas untouched and others mowed, giving the impression of paths through the cemetery gardens. It creates a magical, poetic, almost surreal atmosphere. In the summer, they mow because the grass dries out.\n\n![The cemetery Meadows](2026-05-02-the-cemetery-meadows.jpeg)","x":700,"y":164,"width":380,"height":926,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"c2082de88174bacc","type":"text","text":"# Dancing on Death\n\nVerena tells me that the cemetery is used more like a park; people eat, do yoga on the grass, children play, and even dance. But they don't know that the ground is littered with bones and human bodies. It's a meeting place between life and death.\n\n![Dancing on Death](2026-06-02-dancing-on-death.jpeg)\n","x":1280,"y":411,"width":380,"height":911},
		{"id":"b63995c4444c40b1","type":"text","text":"# Pilgrim of Death\n\nVerena tells me that people go to great lengths to visit certain graves. They sometimes travel from far away. Others come as if they were going to a museum. The cemetery is truly a place rich in activity, a meeting point between life and death. A place of celebration?\n\n![](2026-06-02-pilgrim-of-death.jpeg)\n\n","x":1760,"y":220,"width":380,"height":911},
		{"id":"73fa99715b509b3d","type":"text","text":"# Former Marsh\n\nPlainpalais, Plana Palus in Latin, was a marshy plain, formed by the Arve River. For Jean-Charles, it would be more accurately described as the Plain of the Palace – in the language of birds. A Burgundian palace, referring to the ancient civilization that occupied the territory between 443 and 516.\n\n![Former Marsh](2026-06-02-former-marsh.jpeg)","x":1900,"y":1240,"width":380,"height":926},
		{"id":"fa5eede21ef6f25e","type":"text","text":"# Ancient Temple\n\nJean-Charles, who describes himself as an expert in \"symbolism,\" gives me a guided tour of the cemetery and explains that in the 1800s, there was a hospital and a temple there. He entertains an interesting imagining about what might have become of them. \"Perhaps the temple is underground now? Who knows?\"\n\n![Ancient Temple](2026-06-02-ancient-temple.jpeg)","x":1380,"y":1436,"width":380,"height":926,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"deb5355ed883500b","type":"text","text":"# The Red Grave\n\nWhile strolling through the cemetery, I noticed a grave shape without a number. Everything suggested to me that it was an unofficial, unauthorized, pirate grave. It was even guarded by a small Buddha statue.\n\n![The Red Grave](2026-06-02-the-red-grave.jpeg)","x":2360,"y":558,"width":380,"height":878},
		{"id":"8e3b3b37e7076b6f","type":"text","text":"# Graves without heirs\n\nSome graves have been \"given\" to the city, which is responsible for their upkeep. These graves have no heirs and belong to individuals who had a major positive impact on the city and canton of Geneva. This is the case, for example, of Jean Louis Borjes.\n\n![Graves without heirs](2026-06-02-graves-without-heirs.jpeg)","x":2420,"y":1520,"width":380,"height":902},
		{"id":"918ccc9fb9741241","type":"text","text":"# Rare Moss\n\nRare types of moss grow on graves. A form of new life, a new ecosystem born from the dead. These mosses are protected. Graves are not allowed to be cleaned (except by families). These rare mosses grow because the material of the graves is not native. The more you remove the moss from the graves, the faster and stronger it grows back\n\n![](2026-06-02-rare-moss.jpeg)","x":200,"y":320,"width":380,"height":950,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"979a6aebd15b8d55","type":"text","text":"# Clay soil\n\nGuillem, an agricultural engineer, tells me that in some cases, the soil is so clayey that bodies can no longer decompose. Funeral homes are then forced to exhume the body and cremate it. For the past 20 years, pesticides have been banned in Geneva, and the situation has improved.\n\n![Clay soil](2026-06-02-clay-soil.jpeg)","x":2900,"y":1703,"width":380,"height":926},
		{"id":"d51de17efc57b054","type":"text","text":"# Perennial plants\n\nVerena and Sandrine only plant perennials because they live all year round. They only bloom in the spring, but they never die. They are much more long-lasting than leaves. Despite this, people complain about not having flowers...\n\n![Perenial Plants](2026-06-02-perenial-plants.jpeg)\n\n","x":3460,"y":558,"width":380,"height":878,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"0ebcb514093c391c","type":"text","text":"# Masonic Symbols\n\nJean-Charles tells me that several tombs display Masonic symbols, such as the book, the compass, and the Masonic star. Geneva was apparently an important location for these lodges, which may have had activities in the city's underground, between the Saint-Georges and des Rois cemeteries.\n\n![Masonic Symbols](2026-06-02-masonic-symbols.jpeg)","x":3560,"y":1560,"width":380,"height":926},
		{"id":"40293d6117d5bb2a","type":"text","text":"# Carbon at the Basis of Life\n\nGuillem explains to me that, for him, cremation is probably the most sustainable way to handle bodies after death. For him, the carbon produced by combustion is a kind of return to our roots, since it is the basis of all life.\n\n![Carbon at the Basis of Lifew](2026-06-02-carbon-at-the-basis-of-life.jpeg)","x":4060,"y":760,"width":380,"height":909},
		{"id":"7c117271323ed32b","type":"text","text":"*Softening through an ideal of continuity in a form of beyond. For example with Sempervirens plants that remain always green!*","x":4100,"y":1800,"width":380,"height":115,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"b816ea8e660e34d0","type":"text","text":"*\"It is manifestly impossible to take into account the vulnerability of the Earth, to accord the slightest importance to the fragility of the territories that compose it, as long as we have not acknowledged our own fragility and the structural dependence it reveals with respect to others, human or non-human.\"*","x":10,"y":-30,"width":380,"height":250,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"4b04caf933763e09","type":"text","text":"*List of stories collected during interviews and observations at the Cimetière des Rois.*","x":-440,"y":-400,"width":380,"height":85,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"9b5e7c90d55c8c09","type":"text","text":"*Diversity of life forms at cemeteries or of burial forms*","x":200,"y":2422,"width":380,"height":55,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"847586bacfd6539c","type":"text","text":"*The compost produced can be used as an amendment to enrich the soil or scattered in a natural location*","x":1950,"y":2263,"width":380,"height":85,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"720d93db97f9c772","type":"text","text":"*\"Behold! Life follows death!... and the living organism is a succession of metamorphoses.\" The work of Jacques Brun carries this phrase as its epigraph.*","x":3460,"y":2600,"width":380,"height":145,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"595aa56b97f0f728","type":"text","text":"*Caring for all the living beyond death is participating in the great cycle of life.*","x":2240,"y":296,"width":380,"height":115,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"cd9ff51e50381e96","type":"text","text":"*The fear of death often rests on our need to leave an immortal trace of our passage.*","x":-760,"y":1213,"width":380,"height":85,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"0e8a8bea21d97176","type":"text","text":"*Fear. He is afraid of returning to his normal life, afraid of accepting the absence of that person.*","x":1180,"y":135,"width":380,"height":85,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"b14349d13d91cd67","type":"text","text":"# Raking up fallen leaves\n\nBefore, we used to collect everything and burn it. The fallen leaves, the branches. Now, we move them to the undergrowth. We realize that it's important to let nature do its work. We stop trying to control it.\n\n![Raking up fallen leaves](2026-06-02-raking-up-fallen-leaves.jpeg)","x":2900,"y":676,"width":380,"height":909}
	],
	"edges":[]
}